e memory address of the next instruction to be fetched, incrementing automatically after each fetch",example:"After fetching the instruction at address 100, the PC updates to 101 ready for the next cycle"},{term:"Accumulator (ACC)",definition:"A register in the CPU that temporarily stores the result of arithmetic and logical operations carried out by the ALU",example:"When the ALU calculates 5 + 3, the result 8 is held in the Accumulator before being moved to memory"},{term:"Fetch-Decode-Execute (FDE) Cycle",definition:"The repeated process of fetching an instruction from memory, decoding what it means, then executing it",example:"The CPU fetches an instruction from RAM; the CU decodes it; the CPU carries it out, for example using the ALU to add two values"},{term:"Clock Speed",definition:"The number of CPU clock cycles per second, usually measured in Gigahertz (GHz); a higher clock speed allows more cycles each second",example:"A 3.5 GHz processor has 3.5 billion clock cycles per second; clock speed alone does not determine performance"},{term:"Cores",definition:"Independent processing units within a CPU that can work on instructions concurrently",example:"More cores can speed up work that can be split between them; doubling cores does not necessarily halve running time"},{term:"Cache Size",definition:"The amount of fast cache memory available to store frequently used data and instructions",example:"More cache can reduce fetching from slower RAM, improving performance when the needed data is in cache"},{term:"Embedded System",definition:"A computer built into a larger device to carry out a specific task or a limited set of related tasks",example:"A washing-machine controller runs the wash cycle; a thermostat controls heating; a car's automatic-light controller switches its lights according to light levels"},{term:"Von Neumann Architecture",definition:"A model of computer design where a single memory stores both data and instructions, and the CPU processes them sequentially via the fetch-decode-execute cycle",example:"The CPU fetches instructions from the same memory where data is stored, using registers like MAR, MDR, PC and ACC to manage the process"},{term:"Embedded System Characteristics",definition:"Dedicated hardware and software perform limited functions; the user cannot easily repurpose the system to run unrelated applications",example:"A car's automatic-light controller has a processor dedicated to checking the light sensor and switching the lamps, rather than running general-purpose applications"}],"1.2":[{term:"Random Access Memory (RAM)",definition:"Volatile primary storage that holds data and programs currently in use; contents are lost when power is off",example:"When you open Google Chrome, its program code is loaded from storage into RAM"},{term:"Read Only Memory (ROM)",definition:"Non-volatile primary storage that retains startup or firmware instructions without power; its contents are not normally changed during use",example:"A car controller's ROM can hold its startup instructions and the firmware that controls the system"},{term:"Virtual Memory",definition:"Secondary storage used to hold data moved out of RAM when RAM is full; the OS transfers data back to RAM when needed",example:"An OS moves inactive data to an SSD or HDD to free RAM; accessing secondary storage is slower than accessing RAM"},{term:"Magnetic Storage",definition:"Non-volatile storage that records data magnetically, for example on hard disks or magnetic tape",example:"An HDD uses rotating platters and a read/write head; magnetic tape is often used for large backups"},{term:"Optical Storage",definition:"Uses a laser to read and write data on a reflective disc",example:"Compact Disc (CD), Digital Versatile Disc (DVD), Blu-ray"},{term:"Solid State Storage",definition:"Uses electronic flash memory with no moving parts, making it faster and more durable",example:"Solid State Drive (SSD), Universal Serial Bus (USB) flash drive"},{term:"Bit",definition:"The smallest unit of data in computing; a single binary digit, either 0 or 1",example:"One switch that is either on (1) or off (0)"},{term:"Nibble",definition:"A group of 4 bits",example:"Half a byte, e.g. 1010"},{term:"Byte",definition:"A group of 8 bits",example:"Can represent a whole number from 0 to 255"},{term:"Kilobyte (KB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to 1000 bytes (OCR also accepts 1024)",example:"A small text file might be 50 KB"},{term:"Megabyte (MB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to 1000 kilobytes (OCR also accepts 1024)",example:"A photo might be several MB"},{term:"Gigabyte (GB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to 1000 megabytes (OCR also accepts 1024)",example:"A high-definition film might be 4 GB"},{term:"Terabyte (TB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to 1000 gigabytes (OCR also accepts 1024)",example:"A laptop hard drive might store 1 TB of data"},{term:"Petabyte (PB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to 1000 terabytes (OCR also accepts 1024)",example:"Large cloud providers may store many PB of data"},{term:"Binary",definition:"Base-2 number system using only the digits 0 and 1",example:"01001010 in binary represents 74 in denary"},{term:"Denary",definition:"Base-10 number system using digits 0 to 9; the everyday counting system",example:"The number 255 in denary is 11111111 in binary"},{term:"Hexadecimal",definition:"Base-16 number system using digits 0-9 and letters A-F",example:"2F = 2 x 16 + 15 = 47 in denary; each hex digit maps to four bits, so 2F = 0010 1111"},{term:"Binary Addition",definition:"Adding two binary numbers together, carrying over when a column totals 2 or more",example:"1011 + 0111 = 10010 (11 + 7 = 18 in denary)"},{term:"Overflow",definition:"An error that occurs when the result of a binary calculation needs more bits than are available; the extra bit is lost so the stored answer is incorrect",example:"Adding 11111111 + 00000001 in 8 bits gives 100000000 - the leading 1 is dropped and the result is recorded as 00000000"},{term:"Binary Shift",definition:"Moving every bit a set number of places, filling empty positions with zero and discarding bits shifted out",example:"For unsigned 8-bit values, 00001100 shifted left once becomes 00011000 (12 to 24); 00001101 shifted right once becomes 00000110 (13 to 6). Left shifts double per place only if no significant bit is lost"},{term:"Most Significant Bit (MSB)",definition:"The bit with the highest place value in a binary number, usually the leftmost bit",example:"In 10110001, the MSB is 1 and represents 128"},{term:"Least Significant Bit (LSB)",definition:"The bit with the lowest place value in a binary number, usually the rightmost bit",example:"In 10110001, the LSB is 1 and represents 1"},{term:"Character Set",definition:"A collection of characters with a unique code for each; n bits can represent up to 2^n different codes",example:"7 bits give 128 codes; 8 bits give 256, so representing 150 different characters requires at least 8 bits"},{term:"American Standard Code for Information Interchange (ASCII)",definition:"A character set mapping characters to numeric codes; OCR J277 questions represent ASCII using 8 bits per character (original ASCII used 7 bits)",example:"'A' is 65 (01000001), 'B' is 66 (01000010); consecutive letters have consecutive codes"},{term:"Unicode",definition:"A character set covering many languages and symbols; the number of bits used depends on the encoding",example:"Unicode can represent Hello, Chinese characters and emojis; ASCII cannot represent all of these. Use the stated bits per character in file-size questions"},{term:"Sound Sampling",definition:"Converting analogue sound to digital by measuring amplitude at regular intervals and storing each sample as a binary value",example:"Higher sample rate or bit depth can improve fidelity and increase file size; a longer recording increases file size without improving fidelity"},{term:"Sample Rate",definition:"The number of audio samples taken per second, measured in Hertz (Hz)",example:"CD quality audio uses 44,100 samples per second (44.1 kHz)"},{term:"Bit Depth",definition:"The number of bits used to record each audio sample; more bits means more detail",example:"16-bit audio gives 65,536 possible amplitude levels per sample"},{term:"Pixel",definition:"The smallest individual element of a digital image; each pixel stores a single colour value as a binary code",example:"A 1920 x 1080 image is made up of 2,073,600 pixels arranged in a grid"},{term:"Bitmap Image",definition:"A graphic made up of a grid of pixels, each assigned a colour value",example:"A 1920 x 1080 image contains over 2 million pixels"},{term:"Colour Depth",definition:"The number of bits used for each pixel; n bits allow up to 2^n colours",example:"7 bits allow 128 colours; 8 allow 256, so 240 colours need 8 bits. More bits can improve colour detail but increase file size"},{term:"Resolution",definition:"The dimensions of an image in pixels, usually width x height",example:"A 200 x 100 image has 20,000 pixels. Increasing dimensions can capture more detail but increases file size at the same colour depth"},{term:"Metadata",definition:"Additional data stored alongside an image or sound file describing its properties so the file can be interpreted correctly",example:"An image file's metadata includes width, height, colour depth, date taken and camera model"},{term:"Image File Size Calculation",definition:"File size = width x height x colour depth (in bits), then convert to bytes",example:"100 x 100 pixels at 24-bit colour = 240,000 bits = 30,000 bytes"},{term:"Sound File Size Calculation",definition:"File size = sample rate x bit depth x duration (seconds), then convert to bytes",example:"44,100 x 16 x 10 seconds = 7,056,000 bits = 882,000 bytes"},{term:"Text File Size Calculation",definition:"File size = bits per character x number of characters, then convert bits to bytes if required",example:"A 1,000-character message in 8-bit ASCII = 1,000 x 8 = 8,000 bits = 1,000 bytes (1 KB)"},{term:"Compression",definition:"Reducing the size of a file so it takes up less storage and transfers more quickly across a network; can be lossy or lossless",example:"A 10 MB photo compressed to 2 MB downloads five times faster on the same connection"},{term:"Lossy Compression",definition:"Reduces file size by permanently removing some data that cannot be recovered",example:"Joint Photographic Experts Group (JPEG) removes colour detail the human eye barely notices"},{term:"Lossless Compression",definition:"Reduces file size without losing any data; the original file can be perfectly rebuilt",example:"Portable Network Graphics (PNG), ZIP files; identical data when decompressed"},{term:"Secondary Storage",definition:"Non-volatile storage that keeps programs and files when power is off",example:"A saved document remains on an SSD after shutdown; it is loaded into RAM when opened"},{term:"Choosing Storage",definition:"Compare capacity, speed, cost, portability, durability and reliability against the needs of the user",example:"A portable SSD is fast, compact and resists knocks because it has no moving parts; an HDD may offer more capacity for the same cost, but moving parts are vulnerable to damage"}],"1.3":[{term:"Local Area Network (LAN)",definition:"A network covering a small geographical area, typically owned and managed by one organisation",example:"A school network connecting all computers across one or two buildings"},{term:"Wide Area Network (WAN)",definition:"A network covering a large geographical area, using third-party communication links",example:"The internet connecting users in London to servers in New York"},{term:"Bandwidth",definition:"The maximum amount of data that can be transmitted across a network connection in a fixed time, usually measured in megabits per second (Mbps); a key factor affecting network performance alongside the number of connected devices",example:"A 100 Mbps fibre line shared between 10 users averages 10 Mbps each; a school Wi-Fi slows at break time when many phones connect"},{term:"Star Topology",definition:"A network layout where all devices connect to a central switch or hub",example:"A failed device link leaves other devices connected, but failure of the central switch affects the whole network; adding a device requires a link to the switch"},{term:"Mesh Topology",definition:"A network with multiple direct connections between devices; a full mesh connects every pair, while a partial mesh connects only some pairs",example:"Alternative paths can keep communication working if a link fails; extra connections can make setup more complex"},{term:"Client-Server Network",definition:"A central server provides services and resources; client devices make requests",example:"A school file server holds shared folders that students access from their PCs"},{term:"Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Network",definition:"All devices are equal and share resources directly with each other without a central server",example:"Two laptops sharing files directly over a home network"},{term:"Wired Connection (Ethernet)",definition:"Connecting devices using physical cables and Ethernet standards",example:"A fixed office computer can use a stable, high-bandwidth cable connection, but installing cables costs money and restricts movement"},{term:"Wireless Connection (Wi-Fi)",definition:"Connecting devices using radio waves through a Wireless Access Point (WAP)",example:"A tablet can move within signal range without a cable, but walls, interference and shared bandwidth can reduce performance"},{term:"Bluetooth",definition:"Short-range wireless technology for pairing devices over a few metres",example:"Wireless headphones connecting to a smartphone"},{term:"Internet Protocol (IP) Address",definition:"An address used to identify a device or interface on an IP network and route data to it",example:"IPv4 example: 192.168.1.1. IPv6 example: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001"},{term:"Media Access Control (MAC) Address",definition:"A unique hardware address permanently burned into a device's Network Interface Card (NIC)",example:"00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E; set during manufacturing and cannot normally be changed"},{term:"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)",definition:"A network protocol that checks packets are delivered reliably and reassembles them in the correct order",example:"When a web page is downloaded, TCP can request missing packets again so the page data arrives complete"},{term:"IP (Internet Protocol)",definition:"A network protocol that adds source and destination addresses so packets can be routed between networks",example:"Each packet travelling across the internet includes an IP address for the device it is being sent to"},{term:"HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) / Secure (HTTPS)",definition:"Protocols for requesting and transferring web pages; HTTPS also encrypts the data between the browser and the web server",example:"Visiting https://www.bbc.co.uk; the padlock icon shows HTTPS is active"},{term:"File Transfer Protocol (FTP)",definition:"A protocol used for transferring files between a client and a server",example:"Uploading website files from a local computer to a web hosting server"},{term:"SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)",definition:"The protocol used to send emails from a client to a server, or between mail servers",example:"When you click Send in Gmail, SMTP transfers your email to the recipient's mail server"},{term:"POP (Post Office Protocol)",definition:"An email protocol that downloads messages from the server to the local device and then removes them from the server",example:"Downloading emails to a desktop client using POP means they are no longer accessible from your phone"},{term:"IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)",definition:"An email protocol that keeps messages stored on the server and syncs them across all devices",example:"Reading an email on your phone and it also appearing as read on your laptop uses IMAP"},{term:"Email Protocols",definition:"A set of rules that govern how emails are sent, received, and stored across networks; SMTP handles sending, while POP and IMAP handle receiving",example:"When you send an email, SMTP delivers it to the recipient's server; when they check their inbox, POP or IMAP retrieves it"},{term:"Layering",definition:"The concept of dividing network communication into separate, independent layers where each layer has a specific role and communicates only with the layers directly above and below it",example:"Switching from Ethernet to Wi-Fi only changes the layer that handles the connection; the layers above it, such as the one where HTTP works, are unaffected"},{term:"Network Interface Card (NIC)",definition:"Hardware component that allows a device to connect to a network, either wired or wirelessly",example:"Every laptop has a built-in wireless NIC"},{term:"Router",definition:"A device that forwards packets between different networks and determines the best route",example:"Your home router sends data between your LAN and the internet (WAN)"},{term:"Switch",definition:"A device that connects devices within a LAN and sends data only to the intended recipient using MAC addresses",example:"A 24-port switch in a school server room connecting all classroom PCs"},{term:"Wireless Access Point (WAP)",definition:"A device that allows wireless devices to connect to a wired network",example:"A WAP mounted on a classroom ceiling providing Wi-Fi"},{term:"Transmission Media",definition:"The physical pathways that carry data signals between devices on a network, either wired (cables) or wireless (radio waves)",example:"Copper Ethernet cable, fibre optic cable and Wi-Fi radio waves are all transmission media"},{term:"Domain Name System (DNS)",definition:"A system of Domain Name Servers that converts human-readable domain names into IP addresses so browsers can locate websites",example:"Typing www.bbc.co.uk into a browser; the DNS converts this to an IP address like 151.101.0.81 so the request reaches the correct server"},{term:"The Cloud",definition:"Remote provision of storage, software or processing over a network, usually the internet",example:"Cloud storage allows access from different devices and can reduce local hardware needs, but depends on connectivity and the provider, and may have ongoing costs"},{term:"Hosting",definition:"The provision of server space and resources by a hosting company so that websites, applications or files are continuously available over the internet",example:"A web hosting company stores a school's website files on its servers so they can be reached at any time via the school's domain name"},{term:"Web Server and Client",definition:"A web server stores and delivers web pages; a client is a device or program that requests and displays those pages",example:"A web server hosts the BBC website; your browser acts as the client requesting and displaying the page"},{term:"Standards",definition:"Agreed rules and specifications that allow hardware and software from different manufacturers to work together",example:"Standards ensure a keyboard made by one company works with a computer made by another; they enable interoperability across the industry"},{term:"IP Address Formats",definition:"IPv4 has four decimal groups from 0 to 255 separated by dots. An uncompressed IPv6 address has eight groups of up to four hexadecimal digits separated by colons",example:"192.168.1.1 is valid IPv4; 258.0.0.1 is invalid. 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001 is valid IPv6"}],"1.4":[{term:"Malware",definition:"Malicious software designed to damage systems or gain unauthorised access",example:"Virus, trojan, spyware, ransomware, worm"},{term:"Virus",definition:"Malware that attaches to a legitimate file and spreads when the file is executed",example:"A virus hidden inside a downloaded game installer that corrupts files when run"},{term:"Trojan",definition:"Malware disguised as legitimate software that creates a backdoor for attackers",example:"A free app that secretly installs a keylogger to record passwords"},{term:"Spyware",definition:"Malware that secretly monitors user activity and sends data to a third party",example:"Software tracking every website you visit and sending it to advertisers"},{term:"Ransomware",definition:"Malware that encrypts a victim's files and demands payment for the decryption key",example:"WannaCry attack in 2017 locked hospital computers and demanded Bitcoin"},{term:"Worm",definition:"Self-replicating malware that spreads automatically across networks without needing a host file or user action",example:"A worm exploits a vulnerability in connected computers and copies itself between them, slowing the network as it spreads"},{term:"Phishing",definition:"Fake communication (usually email) designed to trick users into revealing personal data",example:"An email pretending to be from a bank asking you to click a link and enter your login details"},{term:"Brute Force Attack",definition:"Trying every possible password combination systematically until the correct one is found",example:"Software cycling through millions of password combinations per second"},{term:"Denial of Service (DoS) Attack",definition:"Flooding a server with so many requests that it becomes overloaded and unavailable to legitimate users",example:"Overloading an online shop's server so real customers cannot access the website"},{term:"Structured Query Language (SQL) Injection",definition:"Inserting malicious SQL code into a website's input field to access or manipulate the database",example:"Typing ' OR 1=1 -- into a login form to bypass authentication"},{term:"Data Interception and Theft",definition:"Intercepting data as it is being transmitted across a network, often using packet sniffing tools or man-in-the-middle techniques",example:"An attacker on a public Wi-Fi network capturing unencrypted login credentials as they are sent between a laptop and a server"},{term:"Social Engineering",definition:"Manipulating people psychologically into giving up confidential information or access",example:"Phoning an IT helpdesk pretending to be a senior manager to get a password reset"},{term:"Penetration Testing",definition:"An authorised simulated attack carried out to identify security weaknesses before real attackers do",example:"An ethical hacker hired by a company to test whether their firewall can be breached"},{term:"Anti-Malware Software",definition:"Software that scans for, blocks and removes malicious programs such as viruses, trojans and spyware before they can damage the system",example:"Microsoft Defender or Norton scanning incoming downloads and quarantining infected files"},{term:"Passwords",definition:"A secret string of characters used to authenticate a user; strong passwords combine length, mixed case, digits and symbols to resist brute-force attacks",example:"Requiring at least 12 characters with mixed case, digits and symbols, and locking the account after three failed attempts"},{term:"Firewall",definition:"Software or hardware that monitors and filters incoming and outgoing network traffic based on security rules",example:"Blocking incoming connections from unrecognised IP addresses"},{term:"Encryption",definition:"Using an algorithm and key to turn readable data into ciphertext that needs the appropriate key to decrypt",example:"HTTPS encrypts transmitted data so an interceptor cannot understand it without the decryption key; encryption does not prevent interception"},{term:"User Access Levels",definition:"Rules that give each user only the access they need: which files, folders and software they can use, and whether they can read, edit or delete them",example:"A student account cannot install software, but an admin account can"},{term:"Physical Security",definition:"Measures that physically protect computer systems and data from unauthorised access, theft or damage",example:"Locked server rooms, security cameras, biometric door entry systems, cable locks on laptops"}],"1.5":[{term:"Operating System (OS)",definition:"System software that manages hardware resources and provides a platform for application software to run",example:"Microsoft Windows, Apple macOS, Linux, Google Android"},{term:"User Interface",definition:"The means by which the user interacts with the computer system",example:"Graphical User Interface (GUI) uses icons, windows and menus; Command Line Interface (CLI) uses typed text commands"},{term:"Memory Management",definition:"The OS allocates and frees RAM, manages transfers to and from memory, and keeps running programs separate to support multitasking",example:"The OS gives each application memory and can move inactive data to secondary storage when RAM is full"},{term:"Multitasking",definition:"The OS rapidly switches between running programs so they appear to run at the same time",example:"Listening to music on Spotify while typing an essay in Word"},{term:"Peripheral Management",definition:"An operating system function that controls communication with input, output and storage devices",example:"The OS manages sending data to a printer and receiving data from a scanner"},{term:"Drivers",definition:"Software that lets the operating system communicate with and control a specific hardware device",example:"Installing a printer driver so the OS knows how to send documents to print"},{term:"User Management",definition:"The OS controls user accounts, access levels and permissions",example:"An admin account has full access; a student account has restricted permissions"},{term:"File Management",definition:"The OS organises files into folders and directories and controls who can read, write or delete them",example:"Creating a folder called Homework, moving files into it, setting read-only permissions"},{term:"Defragmentation",definition:"A utility that reorganises fragmented files on a Hard Disk Drive (HDD) so they are stored in contiguous blocks",example:"Files accessed faster because the read/write head does not need to jump across the platter"},{term:"Compression Utility",definition:"Software that reduces the size of files for storage or transfer",example:"Zipping a folder of images before emailing it to reduce the attachment size"},{term:"Encryption Utility",definition:"Software that scrambles files or drives so they cannot be understood without the decryption key",example:"Encrypting a USB flash drive with BitLocker so its contents are protected if lost"},{term:"Utility Software",definition:"System software that maintains, protects or optimises a computer",example:"Encryption protects stored data; compression reduces file size; defragmentation speeds access to fragmented files on an HDD"}],"1.6":[{term:"Data Protection Act (DPA) 2018 / General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)",definition:"United Kingdom (UK) law governing how personal data is collected, stored, used and shared; organisations must keep data secure",example:"A school must keep student records secure, only use them for educational purposes, and delete them when no longer needed"},{term:"Computer Misuse Act (CMA) 1990",definition:"UK law making it illegal to gain unauthorised access to computer systems, or to modify data without permission",example:"Hacking into someone's email account is a criminal offence, even if no data is stolen"},{term:"Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988",definition:"UK law protecting creators' intellectual property from being copied, distributed or modified without permission",example:"Illegally downloading music, films or software without paying the creator"},{term:"Open Source Software",definition:"Software supplied with source code and a licence allowing users to inspect, modify and redistribute it under the licence terms",example:"A school can adapt an open-source program to its needs, but needs the skills to maintain changes and must follow the licence conditions"},{term:"Proprietary Software",definition:"Software controlled by its owner; source code is normally unavailable and use or modification is restricted by its licence. It may be free or paid",example:"A school might choose a supported commercial package for vendor support, but cannot normally adapt its source code"},{term:"Software Licence",definition:"A legal agreement that defines how software can be installed, used, copied and distributed; protects the creator's intellectual property and sets the terms of use",example:"A school buying a site licence for Microsoft Office that permits installation on every staff computer for one year"},{term:"Digital Divide",definition:"The gap between people who can access and effectively use digital technology and those who cannot",example:"Moving homework online offers flexible access, but can disadvantage pupils without devices, connectivity or digital skills"},{term:"Privacy",definition:"Control over access to personal information and how it is collected, used and shared",example:"Facial recognition may help identify offenders, but also records innocent people; consider security benefits, privacy, consent and how data is protected"},{term:"Cyberbullying",definition:"Using digital technology to harass, threaten or humiliate someone",example:"Sending abusive messages through social media or group chats"},{term:"Environmental Impact of Technology",definition:"The effects of manufacturing, using and disposing of technology on the environment",example:"Replacing school tablets uses raw materials and creates e-waste; keeping usable devices longer reduces waste, while newer efficient devices may use less power"},{term:"Sustainability",definition:"Designing and using technology in ways that minimise environmental harm",example:"Using renewable energy to power data centres, recycling old devices"}],"2.1":[{term:"Computational Thinking",definition:"Analysing a problem so that it can be solved in a logical way, using abstraction, decomposition and algorithmic thinking",example:"Planning a quiz app before coding: ignore the colours, split it into questions and scoring, then write the steps for each part"},{term:"Abstraction",definition:"Removing unnecessary detail to focus on the important information needed to solve a problem",example:"A London Underground map removes real geography and shows only stations and connections"},{term:"Decomposition",definition:"Breaking a large, complex problem down into smaller, more manageable sub-problems",example:"Building a game: break it into input handling, scoring, graphics, and collision detection"},{term:"Algorithmic Thinking",definition:"Creating a step-by-step set of instructions (an algorithm) to solve a problem",example:"Writing out the steps for making a cup of tea in the exact order a computer would follow"},{term:"Pseudocode",definition:"A way of writing out an algorithm in structured English that resembles code but is not tied to any programming language",example:'IF age >= 18 THEN OUTPUT "You can vote"'},{term:"Flowchart",definition:"A diagram using standard shapes to represent the steps and decisions in an algorithm",example:"Oval = start/stop, rectangle = process, diamond = decision, parallelogram = input/output"},{term:"Structure Diagram",definition:"A hierarchical diagram that shows how a problem has been decomposed into sub-problems",example:"A top box labelled Quiz Program with sub-boxes for Ask Questions, Check Answers, Display Score"},{term:"Trace Table",definition:"A table used to manually track the values of variables as an algorithm is executed step by step",example:"Tracking the value of count through each iteration of a loop to check the output is correct"},{term:"Identifying Errors",definition:"Using trace tables, dry runs or testing to find logical or syntactical mistakes in an algorithm",example:"Tracing a sorting algorithm and spotting that two values are swapped in the wrong order"},{term:"Inputs, Processes, Outputs",definition:"Inputs provide data, processing transforms it, and outputs communicate the result",example:`Python: age = int(input("Age: ")); print(age + 1) inputs an age, calculates next year's age and outputs it`},{term:"Linear Search",definition:"A searching algorithm that checks each item in a list one by one from start to end until the target is found or the list ends",example:"Searching for Zain in an unsorted register by reading every name from the top"},{term:"Binary Search",definition:"A searching algorithm that repeatedly halves a sorted list by comparing the target to the middle item",example:"Searching for 42 in a sorted list: check middle, discard the half that cannot contain 42, repeat"},{term:"Bubble Sort",definition:"A sorting algorithm that repeatedly compares adjacent pairs and swaps them if they are in the wrong order; passes continue until no swaps are needed",example:"List [5, 3, 8, 1]: first pass compares 5-3 (swap), 5-8 (no swap), 8-1 (swap)"},{term:"Merge Sort",definition:"A sorting algorithm that splits the list in half repeatedly until each sub-list has one item, then merges them back together in order",example:"[6,3,8,1] splits into [6,3] and [8,1], then [6],[3],[8],[1], then merges: [3,6],[1,8], then [1,3,6,8]"},{term:"Insertion Sort",definition:"A sorting algorithm that takes one item at a time and inserts it into the correct position within the already-sorted portion of the list",example:"Like sorting a hand of playing cards: pick up each card and slide it into the right place"}],"2.2":[{term:"Variable",definition:"A named storage location in memory whose value can change while the program runs",example:"score = 0; the value of score can be updated as the game progresses"},{term:"Constant",definition:"A named storage location whose value is set once and does not change during the program",example:"VAT_RATE = 0.20; the rate stays the same throughout"},{term:"Local Variable",definition:"A variable defined within a subprogram whose scope is that subprogram",example:"A function's total is local; a local constant such as TAX_RATE is also restricted to that function. In Python, uppercase names indicate constants by convention"},{term:"Global Variable",definition:"A variable defined at program scope and accessible from subprograms, subject to the language's scope rules",example:"A global constant such as MAX_SCORE can be read by functions. In Python, assigning a global variable inside a function requires a global declaration"},{term:"Assignment",definition:"Storing a value in a variable or constant using the assignment operator",example:'name = "Ada" assigns the string to the variable name'},{term:"Sequence",definition:"Instructions executed one after another in the order they are written",example:"Line 1 runs, then line 2, then line 3"},{term:"Selection (IF/ELSE/ELIF)",definition:"A decision point where the program chooses which path to follow based on a condition",example:'IF temperature > 30 THEN OUTPUT "Hot" ELSE OUTPUT "Cold"'},{term:"Iteration (Loops)",definition:"Repeating a block of code either a set number of times or until a condition is met",example:'FOR i = 1 TO 10 (count-controlled) or WHILE answer != "quit" (condition-controlled)'},{term:"Count-Controlled Loop (FOR)",definition:"A loop that repeats a fixed number of times",example:"for i in range(5): runs exactly 5 times"},{term:"Condition-Controlled Loop (WHILE / DO UNTIL)",definition:"A loop controlled by a condition: WHILE checks before the body and may run zero times; DO UNTIL checks after the body and runs at least once",example:"OCR: do ... until valid == true repeats until valid. Python: while not valid: repeats while valid is false"},{term:"Arithmetic Operators",definition:"Symbols used to perform mathematical calculations",example:"OCR notation: +, -, *, /, MOD (remainder), DIV (quotient), ^ (power). Python uses %, // and ** for the last three"},{term:"Comparison Operators",definition:"Symbols used to compare two values, returning True or False",example:"== (equal to), != (not equal to), < (less than), 